{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact clean head 986ac393fe7b4f531440c70031009e42069c1b33, against the four behaviour-path artifacts: internal/kernel 73acb9ddf42a, internal/app 394871e09dbc, internal/service e403978e60f4 and ui ce4baf5109a8.\n\nThe anchoring observation I raised is now closed. The artifact at path request/resident-rebuild-singleflight is retired and succeeded by four artifacts at the exact strings a merge will change, so a later change in any of those areas will flare what rests on it. I had explicitly not blocked on that, having measured it as the established pre-merge shape — 50 of 503 artifacts use branch-like paths, 19 live, including one of my own — so this is better than the discipline required rather than a defect repaired.\n\nThe verdict and its reasoning are unchanged from my report on the identical commit, and I restate the conclusions rather than make anyone follow a chain of retired requests.\n\nArchitecture. Affected layers: kernel, Workroom application, service surface, browser. The kernel-application contract is preserved. Progress and AuditProgress are reporting-only and the code matches that claim — nothing in the kernel reads them back, no verification decision consults them, the existing Load signature is unchanged, and nil is threaded through the pre-existing scanHead call sites. The kernel still understands no application meaning, so docs/reference/architecture.md is not owed a change. That page does not exist at this head because the head predates the commit on main that added it; I read it from main, and the head merges clean over it.\n\nSecurity. Cancellation independence holds: the audit runs on context.Background(), and the flight is cleared under flightMu only while it is still current, so a finishing flight cannot evict its successor. The test asserts the property rather than the mechanism — after the first reader cancels mid-audit, Verified must not regress and Total must not change, which is what an aborted or restarted rebuild would violate. Projection secrecy holds and is tested adversarially: the endpoint is read into a bare map and any key outside running, verified and total fails the test. Serving no projection during a rebuild is correct on a profile change, where the prior checkpoint is invalid by construction. A short-deadline reader gets a deadline error, not a partial projection. Progress is bounded counters. go test -race is silent on all three changed packages, and AuditProgress is atomic throughout, so the lock-free read is sound; Snapshot loads the fields independently and claims only coherent-enough monotonic observation, which is the right claim for a display and not for a verification input. No new exposure: the resident API carries no authentication and /v0/status already serves the whole projection to any loopback reader.\n\nSimplification. Nothing found that does not weaken a condition. The doubled select in SnapshotWithSource should stay — without the leading non-blocking select, Go chooses at random when both channels are ready and a caller whose context expired as the answer arrived would sometimes discard a completed verified snapshot. Holding both flightMu and an atomic pointer is what lets RebuildProgress answer without any lock, which is the only reason the endpoint can respond while the rebuild holds snapshotMu.\n\nReproduced under -race at this commit: TestProfileMismatchRebuildIsSingleFlightAndPublishesAtomically (8.58s), which forces a genuine mismatch by signing a checkpoint under a deliberately different profile and requires a fresh Workspace to reject it and audit from genesis; TestColdAuditReportsItsProgressWhileHoldingTheLock (6.46s); TestMatchingCheckpointDoesNotStartColdAuditProgress (0.55s), the warm-quiet claim. The final projection's frontier matches the independently loaded verification head and depth and contains the last record written, so the joined reader receives the whole answer rather than a prefix.\n\nGates at this exact head: go vet silent, race silent on the three changed packages, full suite passing, make ui-check exit 0 reproducing the committed embed, clean worktree, 6 ahead of main and 5 behind, merges clean.\n\nStanding question, not a finding: RebuildProgress returns running as progress.Started, so between the flight being created and the kernel calling begin() a reader takes the quiet path. It looks harmless and the tests cover the behaviour either side of it.\n\nMerge ordering worth carrying: my own commitment-projection head f83d5cd5 is approved and must not merge before this correction lands, because the workroom-fold@3 bump forces the cold rebuild this makes honest. I declared that interest when I promised the review, and it did not decide the verdict — the evidence did.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:760dbf9478d0acb883d904a66f8e394871e09dbc","head":"986ac393fe7b4f531440c70031009e42069c1b33","verdict":"approved"}}